    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "We Consider these two errors:\n",
        "\n",
        "### **False Positive:**\n",
        "Benign → predicted malignant\n",
        "\n",
        "### **False Negative:**\n",
        "Malignant → predicted benign\n",
        "\n",
        "Assuming the classifier is being used as a screening or diagnostic-support system and that **failing to identify a malignant** case carries substantially greater consequences than referring a benign case for additional evaluation, then the **false negative is the more costly mistake**.\n",
        "\n",
        "That points you toward:\n",
        "\n",
        "Recall / Sensitivity for malignant cases\n",
        "\n",
        "Recall is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$ Recall=\\frac{TP}{TP+FN} $$\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose:\n",
        "\n",
        "100 patients actually have malignant cancer.\n",
        "Model identifies 97.\n",
        "\n",
        "Recall = 97 / 100 = 97%\n",
        "\n",
        "Only 3 malignant cases were missed.\n",
        "\n",
        "Why not just accuracy?\n",
        "\n",
        "Imagine this exaggerated example:\n",
        "\n",
        "1,000 patients\n",
        "\n",
        "950 benign\n",
        "50 malignant\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose a terrible model says:\n",
        "\n",
        "Everyone is benign.\n",
        "\n",
        "Its accuracy would be:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$ \\frac{950}{1000}=95\\% $$\n",
        "\n",
        "That sounds excellent.\n",
        "\n",
        "But malignant recall would be:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$ \\frac{0}{50}=0\\% $$  \n",
        "\n",
        "It failed to identify every malignant case.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "This demonstrates why accuracy can be misleading when the positive class has greater practical importance and/or the classes are imbalanced.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "We would give greater importance to recall (sensitivity) for the malignant class when comparing the models. Our assumption is that the cost of falsely classifying a malignant tumor as benign is substantially greater than the cost of falsely classifying a benign tumor as malignant. Recall measures the proportion of actual malignant cases that the model correctly identifies and directly reflects the model's ability to minimize false negatives. Accuracy alone would not be sufficient because it treats false-positive and false-negative errors equally and may be influenced by the unequal class distribution. We would still examine precision, F1-score, and the confusion matrix to ensure that improved recall is not achieved through an unreasonable number of false positives.\n"
      ],
